
When the task explicitly requests remote computation, use the configured
remote compute node `${COMPUTE_HOST}`${COMPUTE_DESC_LINE} for:
- GPU / CUDA workloads, training, inference benchmarks
- large simulations, large-memory workloads, long compilations
- anything that would slow down the orchestration machine

For explicitly remote tasks, do not move heavy computation back to the
orchestration machine. A configured host does not change the local default.

# Remote workflow

Before hardware-sensitive work, run `remote-info` and inspect the actual
machine state. Do not assume free GPU memory, CPU load, or installed toolchains.

For normal remote commands use:

    remote-run "<command>"

This synchronizes the current project to `${COMPUTE_WORK_DIR}/<project-name>`
on the remote node, executes there, and downloads `outputs/`.

For long-running jobs use:

    remote-submit <job-name> "<command>"

Long-running jobs must write artifacts and logs under `outputs/`.
Fetch completed results with `remote-fetch`; check active jobs with
`ssh ${COMPUTE_HOST} 'tmux ls || true'`.

# Remote safety

- Do not make the remote copy the authoritative source. Edit locally, then
  `remote-sync` / `remote-run`.
- Do not occupy GPUs blindly. Select GPUs explicitly with CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES
  (or the project's GPU reservation tool if `CLAUDE.md` names one).
- Do not kill other users' processes. Do not use sudo. Do not modify drivers,
  kernel modules, or global toolchains.
- Do not delete anything outside `${COMPUTE_WORK_DIR}/<project-name>`.
